WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.386
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4489.

2
00:00:02.386 --> 00:00:04.773
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:04.773 --> 00:00:23.068
<v STEPHANIE DRAGOON>. . . later date to be released in the interview, so if you say something that you don’t want to be released in the next,

4
00:00:23.068 --> 00:00:31.818
like, ten years or twenty years or . . . [ . . . ?] [break in tape] Okay.

5
00:00:31.818 --> 00:00:35.795
Today is July twenty-seventh, 2015.

6
00:00:35.795 --> 00:00:46.136
We’re here at Ms. Velma Carheel’s house on Anderson Street in Westlake, Louisiana.

7
00:00:46.136 --> 00:01:00.455
I’m Stephanie Dragoon with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

8
00:01:00.455 --> 00:01:15.568
And I’m here today with Mr. George Braxton and Ms. Velma Carheel to talk about their memories of Mossville,

9
00:01:15.568 --> 00:01:31.477
Louisiana as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

10
00:01:31.477 --> 00:01:44.205
Thank you all so much for having us, for sharing your stories and experiences with us.

11
00:01:44.205 --> 00:01:58.523
This is our first session together and your second interview, the second one to the oral history day.

12
00:01:58.523 --> 00:02:08.068
I’m going to start off with a few questions about your neighborhood.

13
00:02:08.068 --> 00:02:20.000
What do you remember about the street that you lived on, Old Spanish Trail?

14
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.909
<v GEORGE BRAXTON>What do I remember about it?

15
00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:26.515
<v DRAGOON>About Old Spanish Trail.

16
00:02:26.515 --> 00:02:36.288
<v BRAXTON>Well, I mean, it was . . . I guess a main route between Westlake and Sulphur [Louisiana].

17
00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:48.667
Most of the town of Mossville lied along the north and the south sides of the Old Spanish Trail.

18
00:02:48.667 --> 00:03:00.394
In my early childhood, it was not . . . even though it was a main route between Sulphur and Westlake,

19
00:03:00.394 --> 00:03:12.121
it was not a real busy street in my mind as it have been in years after that.

20
00:03:12.121 --> 00:03:30.364
I guess when I was a kid I could remember times when if it was late at night you may not hear a car for an hour pass,

21
00:03:30.364 --> 00:03:37.530
but now it’s every couple seconds . . . every few minutes, you know.

22
00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:44.045
I’m not sure what else to say about that street.

23
00:03:44.045 --> 00:03:46.000
I mean . . .

24
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:46.667
<v VELMA CARHEEL>What year did they . . .

25
00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:46.867
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

26
00:03:46.833 --> 00:03:48.000
<v CARHEEL>What year did they pave it?

27
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.235
<v BRAXTON>I don't know that.

28
00:03:50.235 --> 00:04:04.203
I do know that as a kid growing up, we heard a lot of stories about the gold being found along the Old Spanish Trail.

29
00:04:04.203 --> 00:04:08.673
That’s a very prevalent thing in this area.

30
00:04:08.673 --> 00:04:18.171
Jean Lafitte was the pirate that was in this area that the Contraband Days is named after.

31
00:04:18.171 --> 00:04:31.021
So there are lots and lots of stories about gold . . . in fact, to the extent that when I was a very young kid,

32
00:04:31.021 --> 00:04:33.815
I don’t recall it personally,

33
00:04:33.815 --> 00:04:46.665
but I was told about . . . my father got up early one morning before day to go to work and he heard noise outside.

34
00:04:46.665 --> 00:05:02.310
It was a lady that was a schoolteacher was outside digging up the yard because she somehow had determined that there was treasures buried at a certain point.

35
00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:10.690
And he caught her out here digging the yard up, trying to find the treasure.

36
00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:21.865
Those stories are very prevalent along the Old Spanish Trail, and some areas off . . . other streets that parallel that area.

37
00:05:21.865 --> 00:05:26.893
My high school was on the Old Spanish Trail.

38
00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:31.922
The swimming pool was on the Old Spanish Trail.

39
00:05:31.922 --> 00:05:39.744
Essentially, all the things that were special to the area: a little burger stand,

40
00:05:39.744 --> 00:05:45.890
grocery store, church, all along the Old Spanish Trail . . . that street.

41
00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:51.477
I don't know what else to say about that street.

42
00:05:51.477 --> 00:05:57.064
We played in that street . . . I mean quite a bit.

43
00:05:57.064 --> 00:06:06.562
It wasn’t a major highway, so we played alongside the road, rode our bicycles along the side.

44
00:06:06.562 --> 00:06:17.737
It was a path to go anywhere that we were going in our area . . . just following the Old Spanish Trail.

45
00:06:17.737 --> 00:06:25.000
<v DRAGOON>So there is a lot of stories about this pirate growing up?

46
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.300
<v BRAXTON>In the area as a whole.

47
00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:37.650
And the Old Spanish Trail, there were stories of gold being buried along the Old Spanish Trail.

48
00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:45.350
It all kind of relates back, like I say, to the pirate, Jean Lafitte.

49
00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.450
<v DRAGOON>Jean Lafitte?

50
00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:47.000
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

51
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:56.350
Jean Lafitte is the pirate that they drew from to create Contraband Days in Lake Charles [Louisiana],

52
00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:01.850
which is one of the biggest festivals in the nation.

53
00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:09.000
<v DRAGOON>Is that why the school’s named . . . or the mascot is the pirates?

54
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:09.519
<v BRAXTON>Right.

55
00:07:09.519 --> 00:07:11.077
I think so.

56
00:07:11.077 --> 00:07:11.596
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

57
00:07:11.596 --> 00:07:12.115
Wow.

58
00:07:12.115 --> 00:07:14.192
I didn’t know that.

59
00:07:14.192 --> 00:07:21.981
<v BRAXTON>How do you know that? [laughs] Yeah, the pirates was the mascot at the school.

60
00:07:21.981 --> 00:07:24.058
I lost my thought.

61
00:07:24.058 --> 00:07:31.846
There was something else I was going to tell you, but it’s not that significant.

62
00:07:31.846 --> 00:07:34.962
<v CARHEEL>Contraband’s normally in April every year.

63
00:07:34.962 --> 00:07:36.000
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

64
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.293
<v CARHEEL>They have a big celebration in Lake Charles.

65
00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:41.902
A two-week celebration.

66
00:07:41.902 --> 00:07:45.659
It lasts two whole weeks . . . every year.

67
00:07:45.659 --> 00:07:53.171
<v DRAGOON>Did you take trips to go to that festival when you were growing up?

68
00:07:53.171 --> 00:07:58.000
<v CARHEEL>Actually, it started after we were grown up.

69
00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:04.182
<v BRAXTON>Well, it started many years ago, but it’s grown to be extremely big deal.

70
00:08:04.182 --> 00:08:12.571
But to be honest with you, black people didn’t connect with it because Jean Lafitte was a slave trader.

71
00:08:12.571 --> 00:08:18.753
So it hasn’t necessarily been that big of a deal for blacks, African Americans.

72
00:08:18.753 --> 00:08:22.727
But, you know, it is what it is. [Laughs]

73
00:08:22.727 --> 00:08:32.000
<v DRAGOON>What kind of stories did you hear about Jean Lafitte growing up in terms of what he did around Mossville?

74
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.602
<v BRAXTON>It mostly was around the gold.

75
00:08:34.602 --> 00:08:42.843
It actually . . . Their stories are rampant around gold being . . . In fact, that’s what I was about to tell you,

76
00:08:42.843 --> 00:08:50.651
that I had a cousin that lived on the Old Spanish Trail that supposedly found some gold bars.

77
00:08:50.651 --> 00:08:54.988
And he left here in the ‘60s and moved on.

78
00:08:54.988 --> 00:09:02.795
He left everything he owned: businesses, he had some rent houses, a cleaner’s, a store and so forth.

79
00:09:02.795 --> 00:09:11.470
He left and we heard he found some gold bars and he just moved on . . . just left it sitting there.

80
00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:15.807
So whether it’s true or not, I’ve heard another story.

81
00:09:15.807 --> 00:09:25.349
My stepfather told me that his son dug up like a clay pot like container, like a quart . . . pint or quart size.

82
00:09:25.349 --> 00:09:33.590
But what happened was, he had an old-time homemade gate on the fence and the gate had started dragging.

83
00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:41.831
He had been telling him to get a shovel and scoop out under that gate so it wouldn’t drag.

84
00:09:41.831 --> 00:09:46.169
So finally he went out there and he did it,

85
00:09:46.169 --> 00:09:56.578
and he hit this clay pot . . . got it up and it was full of these little things that was just right for a slingshot.

86
00:09:56.578 --> 00:10:03.952
He shot them all away at birds before his dad ever knew that he had found anything.

87
00:10:03.952 --> 00:10:12.193
And when he finally saw what he had and inquired about it, he’d already shot all the nuggets away.

88
00:10:12.193 --> 00:10:19.566
Now, that’s the story he told me, and he told it to me as being the truth.

89
00:10:19.566 --> 00:10:26.072
So whether it was true or not, just like the gold bars, I don't know.

90
00:10:26.072 --> 00:10:39.084
Like the lady that was digging in the yard and who told her that that was a spot to dig under that specific tree or how she figured that out,

91
00:10:39.084 --> 00:10:40.386
I don't know.

92
00:10:40.386 --> 00:10:46.892
But I do know the lady was digging in the yard; that story is true.

93
00:10:46.892 --> 00:10:48.627
That’s the truth, though.

94
00:10:48.627 --> 00:10:50.361
And . . . I don't know.

95
00:10:50.361 --> 00:10:55.133
That’s pretty much all I remember about the Old Spanish Trail.

96
00:10:55.133 --> 00:10:56.000
<v DRAGOON>Who . . .

97
00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:59.611
<v BRAXTON>Well, I guess a couple other things.

98
00:10:59.611 --> 00:11:09.930
To the east side of Mossville as I was coming up, there was plants there along the Old Spanish Trail.

99
00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:19.217
And whenever we’d go to Westlake . . . You know, people didn’t move around as quick as they do today.

100
00:11:19.217 --> 00:11:29.535
But we used to go to Westlake and we’d have to pass over this canal and it smelled very horrible.

101
00:11:29.535 --> 00:11:33.146
We used to call it “Stink Gully.”

102
00:11:33.146 --> 00:11:35.726
I ended up working there.

103
00:11:35.726 --> 00:11:41.917
That’s where I worked my whole life is at that same plant.

104
00:11:41.917 --> 00:11:49.656
And I seen that transform into what you would call normal water in the canal.

105
00:11:49.656 --> 00:11:56.879
And to the west side of us was the suburb, unincorporated area of Sulphur.

106
00:11:56.879 --> 00:12:07.713
So Mossville, along the Old Spanish Trail, as you know, was all-black neighborhood with an adjoining white neighborhood to the west,

107
00:12:07.713 --> 00:12:17.000
and basically refineries and plants to the immediate east side of the community on Old Spanish Trail.

108
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:20.103
<v DRAGOON>Where did you say you worked?

109
00:12:20.103 --> 00:12:20.621
<v BRAXTON>Conoco.

110
00:12:20.621 --> 00:12:21.138
<v DRAGOON>Conoco.

111
00:12:21.138 --> 00:12:23.724
What did you do there?

112
00:12:23.724 --> 00:12:25.793
<v BRAXTON>I was in management.

113
00:12:25.793 --> 00:12:28.379
<v DRAGOON>How long were you there?

114
00:12:28.379 --> 00:12:29.931
<v BRAXTON>Almost forty years.

115
00:12:29.931 --> 00:12:32.000
<v DRAGOON>Until you retired?

116
00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:32.786
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

117
00:12:32.786 --> 00:12:35.932
Almost five years ago.

118
00:12:35.932 --> 00:12:41.436
<v DRAGOON>Who lived in your household growing up?

119
00:12:41.436 --> 00:12:50.872
<v BRAXTON>The base household was my mother, my father, my older sister, Faith,

120
00:12:50.872 --> 00:13:02.667
who was her mother . . . that’s Velma’s mother, my younger sister, [Walon?], and my brother, Henderson.

121
00:13:02.667 --> 00:13:10.530
At various times after that, for a number of years,

122
00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:23.897
had some of the . . . my nieces and nephews also lived there with my older sister, her children.

123
00:13:23.897 --> 00:13:28.615
So that basically composed that household.

124
00:13:28.615 --> 00:13:35.692
<v DRAGOON>And how long did you live in that house?

125
00:13:35.692 --> 00:13:41.197
<v BRAXTON>Until I was grown . . . for nineteen years.

126
00:13:41.197 --> 00:13:52.991
<v DRAGOON>In your first interview, you talked about having a lot of family living around you.

127
00:13:52.991 --> 00:14:04.000
What was it like to have . . . growing up with so many relatives nearby?

128
00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:05.983
<v BRAXTON>It was amazing.

129
00:14:05.983 --> 00:14:21.847
I just . . . It’s hard to identify now with families having kids that stay all over the country . . . aunts and uncles . . . because most of mine,

130
00:14:21.847 --> 00:14:27.136
particularly on my mother’s side of the family,

131
00:14:27.136 --> 00:14:42.339
they basically all lived in a circle around our house and not more than . . . none of them lived more than 300 yards away.

132
00:14:42.339 --> 00:14:48.288
It was a large family, her sisters and brothers.

133
00:14:48.288 --> 00:14:59.525
And on my father’s side of the family, the Braxton side of the family, there were . . . well,

134
00:14:59.525 --> 00:15:12.085
a whole subdivision was named after the Braxtons. [Laughs] It was Braxtons all over the place that were relatives.

135
00:15:12.085 --> 00:15:23.322
All but about two of my uncles that were living during the time I was coming up,

136
00:15:23.322 --> 00:15:31.254
all but two lived in the area of Mossville . . . lived in Mossville.

137
00:15:31.254 --> 00:15:35.881
So it was very close family situation.

138
00:15:35.881 --> 00:15:37.864
Everybody was around.

139
00:15:37.864 --> 00:15:47.119
Practically, at one time I think the population of Mossville exceeded two thousand people,

140
00:15:47.119 --> 00:15:55.712
and the greater majority of those was related to me some way, somehow.

141
00:15:55.712 --> 00:16:01.000
<v DRAGOON>What were the occasions for family gatherings?

142
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:14.851
<v BRAXTON>When I was a kid coming up, we used to have, I recall, family reunions basically at my grandparents’ house on my mother’s side.

143
00:16:14.851 --> 00:16:28.126
But everyone lived so close together that a family reunion didn’t have the same meaning that it had for a lot of people,

144
00:16:28.126 --> 00:16:31.589
because you saw them every day.

145
00:16:31.589 --> 00:16:37.360
We really didn’t have that many scheduled, planned family reunions.

146
00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:45.440
It was a reunion every week or every . . . we were right there close together.

147
00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:48.326
<v CARHEEL>Got together for weddings, church.

148
00:16:48.326 --> 00:16:49.480
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, weddings.

149
00:16:49.480 --> 00:17:02.754
The church was, I guess, one of the places that everybody pretty much came together at because the church basically served the community.

150
00:17:02.754 --> 00:17:07.371
It wasn’t like a lot of churches now.

151
00:17:07.371 --> 00:17:14.297
People come from all over the place, which there were a few.

152
00:17:14.297 --> 00:17:27.571
Basically in the church that I came up in, Mount Zion Baptist Church, the oldest church in Mossville, was started by my great-grandfather.

153
00:17:27.571 --> 00:17:35.651
So it was a gathering place where everybody kind of came together every week.

154
00:17:35.651 --> 00:17:42.000
But otherwise, everybody lived within walking distance of each other.

155
00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:47.060
<v DRAGOON>Can you tell me more about Mount Zion?

156
00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:54.650
<v BRAXTON>Mount Zion, like I said, was started by my great-grandfather in 1866.

157
00:17:54.650 --> 00:18:01.607
His name was Griffin Braxton and he was a former slave.

158
00:18:01.607 --> 00:18:13.624
Mount Zion has a cemetery on the property that dates back into the 1800s that I think the church,

159
00:18:13.624 --> 00:18:25.009
the building, the physical building that’s there now, my understanding is that’s the third building on that site.

160
00:18:25.009 --> 00:18:38.291
The original church was destroyed, I understand, by the 1918 storm and another church was built at some point in time.

161
00:18:38.291 --> 00:18:46.513
And then the church that’s there now was built between 1957 and 1959.

162
00:18:46.513 --> 00:18:56.000
I can barely remember the former wooden church that was there prior to that.

163
00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:01.424
<v DRAGOON>And you said Griffin Braxton was your great-grandfather?

164
00:19:01.424 --> 00:19:02.102
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

165
00:19:02.102 --> 00:19:02.780
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

166
00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:08.203
Who made up the congregation at Mount Zion?

167
00:19:08.203 --> 00:19:19.729
<v BRAXTON>It was mostly family members, with the exception of a few people that . . . and most of those,

168
00:19:19.729 --> 00:19:32.610
a great portion of those, were some form of married into some family members of some sort coming up.

169
00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:37.356
It was . . . When I say family members,

170
00:19:37.356 --> 00:19:49.559
it was members from both sides of the family: my mother’s, the Towner family, and the Braxton family.

171
00:19:49.559 --> 00:20:01.763
Then, some others that were related to us that carried different last names: Rigmaidens, Perkins, Vincents . . . oh, God,

172
00:20:01.763 --> 00:20:16.000
it just goes on and on and on . . . but basically all related somehow back to the Braxton side in particular.

173
00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:22.982
<v DRAGOON>And your grandfather was also really involved with the Mount Zion Church?

174
00:20:22.982 --> 00:20:25.309
<v BRAXTON>He started the church.

175
00:20:25.309 --> 00:20:27.636
He was a preacher.

176
00:20:27.636 --> 00:20:29.964
<v DRAGOON>He started the church.

177
00:20:29.964 --> 00:20:30.545
Okay.

178
00:20:30.545 --> 00:20:32.291
<v BRAXTON>Reverend Griffin Braxton.

179
00:20:32.291 --> 00:20:41.018
He also started at least two other churches in the area that still exist today.

180
00:20:41.018 --> 00:20:49.745
I think, but I have not been able to confirm possibly one or two more.

181
00:20:49.745 --> 00:21:00.218
But he started Good Hope in Westlake and he also started what’s now New Emmanuel in Lake Charles.

182
00:21:00.218 --> 00:21:13.600
He was part of the . . . basically it was three different guys that started that church and he was like the lead, I think.

183
00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:20.000
<v DRAGOON>What kind of involvement in the community did he have?

184
00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:24.598
<v BRAXTON>That is something that I really don’t know,

185
00:21:24.598 --> 00:21:35.517
except to say that just the mere fact that he led a group that started at least three churches,

186
00:21:35.517 --> 00:21:42.414
he must have been pretty instrumental . . . must have had pretty good leadership.

187
00:21:42.414 --> 00:21:48.736
But to confirm or something, I don’t really know for sure.

188
00:21:48.736 --> 00:21:57.356
But just the mere fact that he started three churches, I think, says a lot.

189
00:21:57.356 --> 00:21:57.931
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

190
00:21:57.931 --> 00:21:59.080
Let’s see.

191
00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:10.000
So who were some of the community leaders, and what did they do when you were growing up?

192
00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:22.535
<v BRAXTON>The first person that comes to mind is Joshua Rigmaiden who was known as a community leader.

193
00:22:22.535 --> 00:22:29.908
He died when I was a very, very young kid.

194
00:22:29.908 --> 00:22:38.018
I probably was maybe five years old or something like that.

195
00:22:38.018 --> 00:22:44.654
But I recall his funeral and such . . . in particular.

196
00:22:44.654 --> 00:22:54.240
Basically he lived practically across the street from where I was raised at.

197
00:22:54.240 --> 00:23:03.088
When his funeral procession passed, possibly my imagination ran away with me,

198
00:23:03.088 --> 00:23:15.622
because I recall seeing little white ghostly figures with the funeral procession. [Laughs] I’ll never forget that.

199
00:23:15.622 --> 00:23:27.419
You know, it was just . . . I write it up to my imagination running away from me,

200
00:23:27.419 --> 00:23:33.318
because I don’t believe in ghosts and spirits.

201
00:23:33.318 --> 00:23:37.742
But I do clearly recall that.

202
00:23:37.742 --> 00:23:44.378
As far as others, I would say LaSalle Williams.

203
00:23:44.378 --> 00:23:53.963
We called him Coach Williams because he was the coach at the school.

204
00:23:53.963 --> 00:23:59.124
He was thought of as a disciplinarian.

205
00:23:59.124 --> 00:24:10.184
He also, during the summer months, ran the community recreation swimming pool area and everything.

206
00:24:10.184 --> 00:24:13.871
So he was pretty influential.

207
00:24:13.871 --> 00:24:22.719
And probably the pastor of Mount Zion Church, which was Reverend White.

208
00:24:22.719 --> 00:24:23.456
And . . .

209
00:24:23.456 --> 00:24:25.668
<v CARHEEL>[low audio] Prater?

210
00:24:25.668 --> 00:24:27.143
<v BRAXTON>I’m sorry?

211
00:24:27.143 --> 00:24:29.355
<v CARHEEL>[low audio] Prater?

212
00:24:29.355 --> 00:24:35.991
<v BRAXTON>Well, it’s your opinion. [Laughs] That’s, I guess, influential.

213
00:24:35.991 --> 00:24:38.940
Did you say leaders?

214
00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:42.627
Read that question back again.

215
00:24:42.627 --> 00:24:44.101
<v DRAGOON>Oh, yeah.

216
00:24:44.101 --> 00:24:50.000
Who were some of the community leaders?

217
00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:51.620
<v BRAXTON>Community leaders.

218
00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:52.430
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

219
00:24:52.430 --> 00:24:56.480
And what did they do?

220
00:24:56.480 --> 00:25:04.580
<v BRAXTON>I would say that’s my recollection of community leaders, yeah.

221
00:25:04.580 --> 00:25:05.390
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

222
00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:11.870
What was Mount Zion’s role in civic affairs?

223
00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:20.780
Or what was its role in the community, do you think?

224
00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:26.450
<v BRAXTON>Well, it . . . As I was coming up,

225
00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:37.790
I don't know how to say except that . . . it was a central meeting place.

226
00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:47.510
I’m sure that a lot of things were discussed and worked on.

227
00:25:47.510 --> 00:26:00.470
But as far as my recollection, I don’t really recall anything special that the church did.

228
00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:02.900
I really don’t.

229
00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:11.000
<v DRAGOON>What was the role of women in the church?

230
00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:16.781
<v BRAXTON>My recollection is they served as deacons, members of the choir . . .

231
00:26:16.781 --> 00:26:17.307
<v CARHEEL>Deaconess.

232
00:26:17.307 --> 00:26:17.832
<v DRAGOON>Deaconess?

233
00:26:17.832 --> 00:26:18.883
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, deaconess.

234
00:26:18.883 --> 00:26:26.241
I recall when I was a kid that they used to hold mission circles.

235
00:26:26.241 --> 00:26:28.343
They used to come,

236
00:26:28.343 --> 00:26:43.058
mission circle used to rotate around between different women that were members of the church to their home and they would have Bible study and things like that.

237
00:26:43.058 --> 00:26:50.416
I can recall mission circle used to rotate to my mother’s house at times.

238
00:26:50.416 --> 00:26:59.350
I guess there was kind of a formal thing . . . even on mission day, they all wore white.

239
00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:01.453
I can remember that.

240
00:27:01.453 --> 00:27:14.591
And I remember, that’s how I learned how to make coffee is because my mother had a very special way of making coffee, serving coffee.

241
00:27:14.591 --> 00:27:20.372
And the way it would happen, I mean, she made coffee,

242
00:27:20.372 --> 00:27:32.985
but she would have each cup for each individual that was being served, it would be full of boiling hot water, just clear water.

243
00:27:32.985 --> 00:27:37.190
She’d have a coffee pot sitting there, too.

244
00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:40.343
And it had an empty bowl.

245
00:27:40.343 --> 00:27:52.431
As she served you, you would dump your hot water out of your cup into this bowl, then pour your coffee in there.

246
00:27:52.431 --> 00:27:56.109
The purpose was to preheat the cup.

247
00:27:56.109 --> 00:28:03.467
Then you served yourself with sugar and cream, however you chose to do it.

248
00:28:03.467 --> 00:28:08.197
That’s something that I picked up to this day.

249
00:28:08.197 --> 00:28:10.825
I want my coffee hot.

250
00:28:10.825 --> 00:28:16.080
I still heat my cup if I’m doing it myself.

251
00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:21.861
I like to drink it boiling hot straight from the pot.

252
00:28:21.861 --> 00:28:25.015
If it’s bubbling, that’s just fine.

253
00:28:25.015 --> 00:28:35.000
A lot of people think . . . don’t understand how that happens, but I’ve been doing it all my life.

254
00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:45.452
<v CARHEEL>Their purpose was it was china and china burns easily so it needs to be preheated.

255
00:28:45.452 --> 00:29:00.476
<v BRAXTON>Other than that, I don’t really recall . . . and maybe I was too young to recall some of the things the church did especially.

256
00:29:00.476 --> 00:29:12.887
I think at one time, prior to my time, I understand the church had started a Boy Scout troop.

257
00:29:12.887 --> 00:29:33.137
I was in a Boy Scout troop as a young teenager for a short period of time that was made up of all . . . all the scouts were from the Mossville area.

258
00:29:33.137 --> 00:29:41.629
As far as women’s roles in the church, that’s kind of my recollection.

259
00:29:41.629 --> 00:29:47.508
I don’t recall a whole lot more than that.

260
00:29:47.508 --> 00:29:56.000
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of stuff would you do with your Boy Scout troop?

261
00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:06.934
<v BRAXTON>Well I stayed a short time, because I can only actually remember going out and what we called camping one time,

262
00:30:06.934 --> 00:30:18.388
but basically it was just a daylight event where we went out and we built a campfire and we roast some weenies.

263
00:30:18.388 --> 00:30:34.008
I think at that point in time . . . We had an incident that took place and I did not have a lot of interest in going back to the Boy Scouts.

264
00:30:34.008 --> 00:30:40.256
So it was about the same time that I actually started working.

265
00:30:40.256 --> 00:30:45.983
I had a fulltime job since I was fourteen years old.

266
00:30:45.983 --> 00:30:53.793
So I never had interest in the Boy Scouts after that because I was working.

267
00:30:53.793 --> 00:30:56.397
I had a fulltime job.

268
00:30:56.397 --> 00:30:59.000
<v DRAGOON>What was the incident?

269
00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:05.240
<v BRAXTON>Well, the incident was we had . . . A big deal with the Boy Scouts was discipline.

270
00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:12.729
I can’t tell you what the guys did to require that they be disciplined; I don’t recall that.

271
00:31:12.729 --> 00:31:16.473
But the way they were disciplined, it was self-discipline.

272
00:31:16.473 --> 00:31:18.969
So you formed a belt line.

273
00:31:18.969 --> 00:31:36.443
You formed a line with all of the members facing each other and the person that needed discipline would walk between the lines and you would have to hit them with your belt on the cheek of the butt below the back,

274
00:31:36.443 --> 00:31:40.603
but on the cheek . . . above the thigh, below the waist.

275
00:31:40.603 --> 00:31:44.347
That was a firm rule on where you hit.

276
00:31:44.347 --> 00:31:55.164
They had this one guy that was substantially older than myself that was in the troop and he came through the line and as he passed,

277
00:31:55.164 --> 00:32:00.156
he got hit in the small of the back with the belt.

278
00:32:00.156 --> 00:32:04.317
It wasn’t me, but it was the guy opposite me.

279
00:32:04.317 --> 00:32:15.134
But when you’re hitting like that, somehow he thought it was me that did it, but really it was this other guy on the opposite side.

280
00:32:15.134 --> 00:32:22.622
And he ran to the corner of the room immediately, without hesitation to what I said or anything,

281
00:32:22.622 --> 00:32:29.279
and grabbed a one-half inch galvanized water pipe and was fixing to take my head off,

282
00:32:29.279 --> 00:32:35.519
except that I had some cousins that was in there and they jumped on him.

283
00:32:35.519 --> 00:32:39.263
I had no idea what was even going on.

284
00:32:39.263 --> 00:32:48.000
So they escorted me home and that was it for the Boy Scouts. [Laughs] That was the end of that.

285
00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:52.364
<v DRAGOON>So who were the leaders of the Boy Scouts?

286
00:32:52.364 --> 00:32:54.788
Were they also Mossville folks?

287
00:32:54.788 --> 00:32:56.727
<v BRAXTON>Were they also who?

288
00:32:56.727 --> 00:32:59.636
<v DRAGOON>Mossville folks, who were the leaders?

289
00:32:59.636 --> 00:33:03.030
<v BRAXTON>Yes, the troop was entirely Mossville folks.

290
00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:06.909
You know, I’m not a hundred percent sure.

291
00:33:06.909 --> 00:33:13.212
I’m trying to recall now who the troop leader was at the time,

292
00:33:13.212 --> 00:33:20.485
but I think it was LaSalle Reeves . . . I think, but I won’t swear to that.

293
00:33:20.485 --> 00:33:33.091
It was at a period of time that we used to meet at a building that was pretty much on the site of the recreation center.

294
00:33:33.091 --> 00:33:36.970
We used to call it the Masonic Hall.

295
00:33:36.970 --> 00:33:40.848
That’s where the troops would meet at there.

296
00:33:40.848 --> 00:33:46.667
That’s what kind of ended my stint with the Boy Scouts. [Laughs]

297
00:33:46.667 --> 00:33:51.030
<v DRAGOON>You said you were working since you were fourteen?

298
00:33:51.030 --> 00:33:52.000
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

299
00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:54.345
<v DRAGOON>What did you start doing?

300
00:33:54.345 --> 00:33:56.221
Where were you working?

301
00:33:56.221 --> 00:34:07.008
<v BRAXTON>I used to work at sort of what I think today they would call it a bellhop, at the Pelican Motel in Sulphur.

302
00:34:07.008 --> 00:34:13.574
That was the first real job that I had at a place of business.

303
00:34:13.574 --> 00:34:21.078
But even prior to that, I had little jobs that I picked up in the neighborhood.

304
00:34:21.078 --> 00:34:27.174
I pulled and straightened nails from used lumber . . . and notice I said straighten.

305
00:34:27.174 --> 00:34:32.802
When I say straighten, I had pretty much, on this one job,

306
00:34:32.802 --> 00:34:40.306
I had pretty much had a five-gallon bucket full of nails that I had straightened out.

307
00:34:40.306 --> 00:34:44.996
I had to pull them first out of the wood.

308
00:34:44.996 --> 00:34:47.810
And I used to clean bricks.

309
00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:53.907
I used to clean bricks . . . used bricks, and two bricks for a penny.

310
00:34:53.907 --> 00:34:58.128
So that was the two jobs that I had.

311
00:34:58.128 --> 00:35:00.473
I always wanted to hustle.

312
00:35:00.473 --> 00:35:07.039
In fact I used to . . . I don't know how I ended up doing it,

313
00:35:07.039 --> 00:35:15.012
but I remember that I used to catch bullfrogs at a pond that was near our house.

314
00:35:15.012 --> 00:35:26.267
It wasn’t that terribly many that I caught, and I used to sell them to my teacher at school by the pair, the legs.

315
00:35:26.267 --> 00:35:34.240
It seemed like to me I used to get . . . that was a lot of money back then.

316
00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:40.337
I used to get like two dollars a pair for two little legs.

317
00:35:40.337 --> 00:35:44.558
You couldn’t buy bullfrog legs in the store then.

318
00:35:44.558 --> 00:35:53.000
That was . . . And I don't remember how I actually started doing that, but I remember doing it.

319
00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:56.599
I used to pick blackberries and mayhaws.

320
00:35:56.599 --> 00:36:06.367
We would walk like three or four miles and pick five or six gallons, maybe, of mayhaws or blackberries.

321
00:36:06.367 --> 00:36:24.362
We would walk back that same number of miles with those berries and then walk another mile to the main highway and sit on the side of the road and we would sell those berries.

322
00:36:24.362 --> 00:36:35.158
In fact, when I quit selling them, I think I was getting about three dollars a gallon for berries back then.

323
00:36:35.158 --> 00:36:38.243
That was something that everybody did.

324
00:36:38.243 --> 00:36:45.441
That was . . . You don’t think about it as hard work now, but that was.

325
00:36:45.441 --> 00:36:51.096
They had some people that actually made a living doing that.

326
00:36:51.096 --> 00:36:56.237
It wasn’t a living for me, it was extra change.

327
00:36:56.237 --> 00:37:06.006
But I know there was a cousin of mine that that was his existence, was picking mayhaws and blackberries.

328
00:37:06.006 --> 00:37:16.802
That pretty much defined my working life [Laughs] since I was fourteen, when I actually had a formal job at fourteen.

329
00:37:16.802 --> 00:37:24.000
In between time I did some of these other things, and before that.

330
00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:31.095
Also when I was a teen, when I left working at the Pelican Motel,

331
00:37:31.095 --> 00:37:42.750
I worked at what was at the time known as the best restaurant hotel in the area, which was called the Chateau Charles.

332
00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:45.284
It was a Sheraton Motel.

333
00:37:45.284 --> 00:37:50.351
I worked there in the kitchen for quite a while.

334
00:37:50.351 --> 00:37:56.939
I used to seem like I never had a problem going to work.

335
00:37:56.939 --> 00:37:59.980
I didn’t have a car, okay?

336
00:37:59.980 --> 00:38:04.034
I used to catch a ride, no problem.

337
00:38:04.034 --> 00:38:17.209
You could catch a ride so easy that you never had to worry about it to get back and forth to work and be on time,

338
00:38:17.209 --> 00:38:19.236
and maintain a job.

339
00:38:19.236 --> 00:38:31.399
I used to thumb, catch rides with various people I knew that were going the same way or possibly working at the same place.

340
00:38:31.399 --> 00:38:35.959
In other cases, you knew the Greyhound bus schedule.

341
00:38:35.959 --> 00:38:45.588
The Greyhound bus would pass, and you knew the schedule when they was passing and you could stop them.

342
00:38:45.588 --> 00:38:55.723
Try stopping one now. [Laughs] You could stop it, I could catch a ride to Lake Charles for fifty cents.

343
00:38:55.723 --> 00:39:03.324
During my childhood, also, the city bus, the Lake Charles City bus, came to Mossville.

344
00:39:03.324 --> 00:39:12.953
For roughly ten cents, you could ride the city bus from Mossville back to Lake Charles, back and forth.

345
00:39:12.953 --> 00:39:19.034
So I had a number of different ways that I found transportation.

346
00:39:19.034 --> 00:39:26.128
Now I’m sure y’all have been here and . . . Y’all have eaten at Steamboat Bill’s?

347
00:39:26.128 --> 00:39:28.662
Well, I worked there, too.

348
00:39:28.662 --> 00:39:33.223
That building, it wasn’t Steamboat Bill’s at the time.

349
00:39:33.223 --> 00:39:36.264
It was called the Lakeview Restaurant.

350
00:39:36.264 --> 00:39:41.838
But when I was a teen, I worked there as well.

351
00:39:41.838 --> 00:39:48.932
I was like a busboy, they called it, at that same building, different business.

352
00:39:48.932 --> 00:39:51.973
So I had lots of jobs.

353
00:39:51.973 --> 00:39:54.000
I stayed employed.

354
00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:56.364
<v DRAGOON>You were busing at Steamboat Bill’s?

355
00:39:56.364 --> 00:39:57.152
Did you . . .

356
00:39:57.152 --> 00:40:02.273
<v BRAXTON>I was . . . It’s the same building as Steamboat Bill’s is located in now.

357
00:40:02.273 --> 00:40:05.818
But the name of the business was The Lakeview . . .

358
00:40:05.818 --> 00:40:07.000
<v DRAGOON>The Lakeview.

359
00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:10.312
<v BRAXTON>. . . The Lakeview Motel and Restaurant.

360
00:40:10.312 --> 00:40:14.288
<v DRAGOON>Is that also in Lake Charles?

361
00:40:14.288 --> 00:40:14.950
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

362
00:40:14.950 --> 00:40:20.912
<v DRAGOON>Were you cooking at the other restaurant . . . The Chateau . . . ?

363
00:40:20.912 --> 00:40:26.212
<v BRAXTON>I never officially cooked at any of them.

364
00:40:26.212 --> 00:40:32.175
But I learned to cook working at those places.

365
00:40:32.175 --> 00:40:32.838
Yeah.

366
00:40:32.838 --> 00:40:38.800
I learned a lot about cooking . . . just from observing.

367
00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:51.388
I can actually still remember a couple of very special recipes that was unique to the Chateau Charles restaurant.

368
00:40:51.388 --> 00:40:56.688
I cook them every once in a while.

369
00:40:56.688 --> 00:41:00.000
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of recipes?

370
00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:02.518
<v BRAXTON>One is called shrimp Dijon.

371
00:41:02.518 --> 00:41:15.613
It’s a shrimp dish that basically you cook it in a serving size and it’s made with fresh shrimp and garlic and Worcestershire sauce and such,

372
00:41:15.613 --> 00:41:20.146
and it’s kind of like broiled in an oven.

373
00:41:20.146 --> 00:41:23.672
I learned a lot about fileting fish.

374
00:41:23.672 --> 00:41:33.745
There would be some time I would filet maybe two pickup truck loads of fish in a night . . . large fish,

375
00:41:33.745 --> 00:41:36.263
what we call red fish.

376
00:41:36.263 --> 00:41:41.299
Some of them weigh twenty plus pounds each and such.

377
00:41:41.299 --> 00:41:50.869
I just learned and picked up a lot of tips about cooking, even though I wasn’t a cook there.

378
00:41:50.869 --> 00:41:56.912
The same with the other restaurant that I worked at, the Lakeview.

379
00:41:56.912 --> 00:42:09.000
I was a busboy but I spent a lot of time around the kitchen and such, and picked up a lot of things.

380
00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:12.284
<v DRAGOON>Did you ever cook with your mom?

381
00:42:12.284 --> 00:42:13.691
<v BRAXTON>You know what?

382
00:42:13.691 --> 00:42:18.381
I never cooked with my mom but one time, once,

383
00:42:18.381 --> 00:42:26.825
and that was simply because I said I want to get her lemon meringue pie recipe down pat.

384
00:42:26.825 --> 00:42:34.330
So I told her that we were going to cook lemon pies and I bought everything.

385
00:42:34.330 --> 00:42:41.366
We stood side by side and made those pies and I wrote the recipe down.

386
00:42:41.366 --> 00:42:44.649
I still have it, the original writing.

387
00:42:44.649 --> 00:42:49.809
We made those pies and I’ve been using that recipe since.

388
00:42:49.809 --> 00:42:51.686
Other than that, never.

389
00:42:51.686 --> 00:42:58.253
Not one single time can I ever remember cooking with my mom besides that.

390
00:42:58.253 --> 00:43:04.820
But what I did do, and I think now how did I do that,

391
00:43:04.820 --> 00:43:10.448
but when I was about . . . somewhere between ten and twelve years old,

392
00:43:10.448 --> 00:43:17.485
my father had died when I was ten and my mother took over the business.

393
00:43:17.485 --> 00:43:22.175
I just felt like my mama was working so hard,

394
00:43:22.175 --> 00:43:30.149
because prior to that she was a stay-at-home mom, and now she was gone all the time.

395
00:43:30.149 --> 00:43:33.433
I felt like she was working hard.

396
00:43:33.433 --> 00:43:40.000
She had to come home and then try to cook and all that.

397
00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:43.080
So somehow I acquired . . . had some money.

398
00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:50.120
I went to the little local store, which was only about 200 yards behind our house.

399
00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:54.960
Even though it was a small store it was full service.

400
00:43:54.960 --> 00:44:01.560
And I bought some steak, because I didn’t know nothing about steak I know now.

401
00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:05.080
But steak was supposed to be good, okay?

402
00:44:05.080 --> 00:44:13.440
So I bought some steak, I bought some rice, and I bought some corn, and I cooked a meal.

403
00:44:13.440 --> 00:44:17.400
I had never ever watched, that I could recall.

404
00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:24.000
It was just something I connected with the taste of things she had cooked before,

405
00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:30.160
and in my mind I tried to do whatever it took to mimic it.

406
00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:32.800
I cooked it, and she came.

407
00:44:32.800 --> 00:44:38.080
I can’t tell you how she enjoyed it, but she ate it.

408
00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:42.920
Okay? [Laughs] I’m sure she told me that it was good.

409
00:44:42.920 --> 00:44:49.960
But in the years after that, I used to cook for my mother all the time,

410
00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:57.000
yeah . . . different things . . . soups and whatever, squirrels [Laughs] . . . different things over the years after that.

411
00:44:57.000 --> 00:45:01.667
<v DRAGOON>You said your mother took over the business when your father passed?

412
00:45:01.667 --> 00:45:02.056
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

413
00:45:02.056 --> 00:45:04.000
<v DRAGOON>What was the business?

414
00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:14.235
<v BRAXTON>We had . . . basically we had . . . that’s an area that’s now a part of the city limits of Sulphur.

415
00:45:14.235 --> 00:45:20.490
At the time, it was unincorporated areas called Maplewood and Hollywood.

416
00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:24.471
They’re still referred to by those names.

417
00:45:24.471 --> 00:45:32.431
They were pretty much the well-to-do areas of the Sulphur area, Maplewood and Hollywood.

418
00:45:32.431 --> 00:45:39.824
Maplewood was started by Cities Service Refinery, which is now known as CITGO.

419
00:45:39.824 --> 00:45:51.196
They actually were responsible for building literally hundreds of houses there for people to stay there, supported the plant operation.

420
00:45:51.196 --> 00:46:00.294
So all that incorporated area of Maplewood in Sulphur, my father had the garbage collection business.

421
00:46:00.294 --> 00:46:05.980
He had people that worked for him picking up garbage.

422
00:46:05.980 --> 00:46:16.784
When he passed, she tried to continue the business, but it was a lot of things going against that,

423
00:46:16.784 --> 00:46:25.882
such as other people trying to get it franchised . . . because women didn’t have businesses like that.

424
00:46:25.882 --> 00:46:32.137
She was breaking new ground to have that kind of business.

425
00:46:32.137 --> 00:46:42.373
Also, the city was trying to incorporate some of those areas, too, at the same time, I think.

426
00:46:42.373 --> 00:46:47.490
So after a few years, the business went bust.

427
00:46:47.490 --> 00:46:50.902
It just kind of was inevitable.

428
00:46:50.902 --> 00:46:54.314
The city basically took it over.

429
00:46:54.314 --> 00:47:00.000
<v DRAGOON>And that was when you were about ten to . . .

430
00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:04.741
<v BRAXTON>Well, my father died when I was ten.

431
00:47:04.741 --> 00:47:12.444
But somewhere in the next three, four, five years, the business went bust.

432
00:47:12.444 --> 00:47:18.370
<v DRAGOON>What did she do after that to support the family?

433
00:47:18.370 --> 00:47:21.333
<v BRAXTON>She was just a housewife.

434
00:47:21.333 --> 00:47:23.111
<v DRAGOON>Did she remarry?

435
00:47:23.111 --> 00:47:29.037
<v BRAXTON>In her later . . . Yeah, she did some years after that.

436
00:47:29.037 --> 00:47:39.111
In the latter part of the ‘60s, she married for a short period of time and divorced.

437
00:47:39.111 --> 00:47:45.037
Then she remarried again in the ‘80s until he passed.

438
00:47:45.037 --> 00:47:48.000
So yeah, she remarried.

439
00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:50.000
<v CARHEEL>On her seventy-fifth birthday

440
00:47:50.000 --> 00:47:50.500
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

441
00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:51.000
<v BRAXTON>What?

442
00:47:51.000 --> 00:47:54.000
<v CARHEEL>She remarried on her seventy-fifth birthday.

443
00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:58.000
<v BRAXTON>Oh, yeah, that was a big day, yeah.

444
00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:01.000
It was not only her birthday.

445
00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:04.000
The day she got married was . . .

446
00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:05.000
<v CARHEEL>Thanksgiving.

447
00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:06.895
<v BRAXTON>It was Thanksgiving.

448
00:48:06.895 --> 00:48:12.579
It was John Kennedy’s . . . Was it the assassination date?

449
00:48:12.579 --> 00:48:13.842
<v CARHEEL>Was it?

450
00:48:13.842 --> 00:48:16.368
It was in November.

451
00:48:16.368 --> 00:48:18.263
<v BRAXTON>It was November.

452
00:48:18.263 --> 00:48:33.421
And it was her birthday, her wedding day, Thanksgiving and it was all the same day. [laughs] So it was a pretty big day.

453
00:48:33.421 --> 00:48:37.211
<v CARHEEL>She had a big church wedding.

454
00:48:37.211 --> 00:48:41.000
<v DRAGOON>Sounds like quite the day.

455
00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:47.600
<v BRAXTON>And she was, I think she was . . . How old was she?

456
00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:48.200
<v CARHEEL>Seventy-five.

457
00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:51.200
<v BRAXTON>I don't think she was.

458
00:48:51.200 --> 00:48:54.800
She wasn’t seventy-five, I don't think.

459
00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:57.200
No, she wasn’t seventy-five.

460
00:48:57.200 --> 00:48:59.000
I don’t believe.

461
00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:00.800
Maybe she was.

462
00:49:00.800 --> 00:49:03.200
I forgot the year.

463
00:49:03.200 --> 00:49:05.000
Time gets away.

464
00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:08.000
<v CARHEEL>I don't remember, either.

465
00:49:08.000 --> 00:49:09.734
<v BRAXTON>I don't know.

466
00:49:09.734 --> 00:49:11.468
Not right off . . .

467
00:49:11.468 --> 00:49:12.624
<v DRAGOON>Let’s see.

468
00:49:12.624 --> 00:49:18.981
Can you tell me a little bit more about your grandparents?

469
00:49:18.981 --> 00:49:24.760
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, I was thinking about that a little bit earlier.

470
00:49:24.760 --> 00:49:33.430
On my mother’s side of the family, my great-grandfather on that side of the family,

471
00:49:33.430 --> 00:49:40.365
his name was Israel Towner and he hailed from Beauregard Parish [Louisiana].

472
00:49:40.365 --> 00:49:46.144
Of course he was born in the middle of 1800s.

473
00:49:46.144 --> 00:49:55.392
He was good friends with the father of the governor of the state, Sam Houston Jones.

474
00:49:55.392 --> 00:50:06.951
In fact, there was an article in McNeese [State University] that speaks to the first Beauregard Parish Fair they had,

475
00:50:06.951 --> 00:50:15.042
and where Sam Houston Jones’ father was telling everybody the history of the parish,

476
00:50:15.042 --> 00:50:24.289
because he was . . . he was like . . . for lack of another term, the father of the parish.

477
00:50:24.289 --> 00:50:28.335
He was one of the first settlers.

478
00:50:28.335 --> 00:50:34.692
Nobody expected anybody to know more about Beauregard Parish than him.

479
00:50:34.692 --> 00:50:41.049
So he told the history at this first Beauregard Parish Fair.

480
00:50:41.049 --> 00:50:55.498
At the end of his speech he said that if anybody wanted to know more about the parish, they could ask him after his speech,

481
00:50:55.498 --> 00:51:08.791
and if he could not give the answer, he said . . . this was almost a quote, “My good colored friend, Israel Towner, can answer.

482
00:51:08.791 --> 00:51:15.726
And if neither of us can answer this question, you’ll never know.”

483
00:51:15.726 --> 00:51:19.772
Like I said, I found that article.

484
00:51:19.772 --> 00:51:31.331
Actually the newspaper in Beauregard Parish had at one point did a big two-page spread that had that in it.

485
00:51:31.331 --> 00:51:40.000
I know it’s in McNeese library now because I got it from McNeese library.

486
00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:48.589
So he was . . . My great-grandfather was a pretty influential man in his own right in Beauregard Parish.

487
00:51:48.589 --> 00:51:59.200
And at one point in time, he . . . I’m told that he owned part of the property that’s now Bundick Lake [Deridder,

488
00:51:59.200 --> 00:52:10.821
LA] and he lost it over a dollar and sixty cents’ worth of taxes that he asked a friend of his to pay,

489
00:52:10.821 --> 00:52:14.863
and the friend failed to pay the taxes.

490
00:52:14.863 --> 00:52:17.389
And he lost the property.

491
00:52:17.389 --> 00:52:29.011
Well, I don't know how much property that was, but I do know that, because I confirmed this and found the records myself,

492
00:52:29.011 --> 00:52:37.095
he was given an eighty-acre land grant in the latter 1800s, 1892, thereabouts in that area.

493
00:52:37.095 --> 00:52:39.621
So he had property there.

494
00:52:39.621 --> 00:52:46.189
And they had . . . We had a family reunion where . . . up in Deridder [Louisiana],

495
00:52:46.189 --> 00:52:52.253
where some family members actually brought his tax receipt and showed it.

496
00:52:52.253 --> 00:52:58.316
It was one of the things they showed at the family reunion.

497
00:52:58.316 --> 00:53:00.842
He didn’t make that up.

498
00:53:00.842 --> 00:53:05.389
That was in the middle of the ‘80s, thereabouts.

499
00:53:05.389 --> 00:53:13.474
And he never . . . As far as I know, he always lived in the Beauregard Parish area.

500
00:53:13.474 --> 00:53:16.000
He was buried there.

501
00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:25.807
So his son, my grandfather, they migrated to the Mossville area from Beauregard Parish.

502
00:53:25.807 --> 00:53:39.117
My great-grandfather on the Braxton side, Griffon Braxton, I know for a fact that he was a former slave.

503
00:53:39.117 --> 00:53:46.122
I actually have found his name on a slave manifest.

504
00:53:46.122 --> 00:54:00.832
Back at that time, slaves didn’t have . . . not all slaves, or very few slaves had a first and a last name.

505
00:54:00.832 --> 00:54:16.244
But I found his name on the slave manifest for August the twenty-seventh, 1840 coming into the port of New Orleans [Louisiana].

506
00:54:16.244 --> 00:54:43.563
I’m trying to work the time to understand where he was at between 1840 and a period of time when he lived in the area south of him called Vincent Settlement before he moved from Vincent Settlement to Mossville.

507
00:54:43.563 --> 00:54:49.168
Vincent Settlement is an area south of Sulphur.

508
00:54:49.168 --> 00:54:53.371
It’s not that far from here.

509
00:54:53.371 --> 00:55:04.579
I mean, it’s like maybe ten, fifteen-mile drive just to get there, maybe, if that much.

510
00:55:04.579 --> 00:55:13.685
But I do know he lived in that area before he moved here.

511
00:55:13.685 --> 00:55:24.893
My grandmother on my father’s side of the family, her maiden name, she was a Rigmaiden.

512
00:55:24.893 --> 00:55:34.000
I think that she was born and raised in the Mossville area.

513
00:55:34.000 --> 00:55:50.342
Now, my grandmother on my mother’s side of the family, I think she came from Beauregard Parish as well . . . the Beauregard Parish area.

514
00:55:50.342 --> 00:55:56.737
She was a Potts, P-O-T-T-S was her maiden name.

515
00:55:56.737 --> 00:56:03.132
My great-grandfather’s wife . . . I’m having difficulty recalling her name.

516
00:56:03.132 --> 00:56:15.211
I get those . . . the women’s names mixed up for some reason, so I’m not going to say.

517
00:56:15.211 --> 00:56:18.053
What else about them?

518
00:56:18.053 --> 00:56:20.184
I don't know.

519
00:56:20.184 --> 00:56:22.316
I really don’t.

520
00:56:22.316 --> 00:56:36.526
My grandfather on . . . My grandparents both on my father’s side of the family, they passed on before I was born.

521
00:56:36.526 --> 00:56:47.895
On my mother’s side of the family, they were both living and I can recall them.

522
00:56:47.895 --> 00:57:02.105
My grandfather on my mother’s side passed when I was somewhere around five years old, but I clearly recall him.

523
00:57:02.105 --> 00:57:06.368
But my grandmother lived until 1973.

524
00:57:06.368 --> 00:57:14.895
And every son, all the sons they had . . . what was it, five?

525
00:57:14.895 --> 00:57:20.579
Arthur, Frank, Frederick, Johnny . . . Who am I missing?

526
00:57:20.579 --> 00:57:30.526
All of their sons were carpenters, and I mean first-class carpenters, all of them.

527
00:57:30.526 --> 00:57:35.500
They often worked together as a team.

528
00:57:35.500 --> 00:57:42.605
These are . . . all them I was raised up around them.

529
00:57:42.605 --> 00:57:53.263
On my daddy’s side of the family, my father’s brother actually worked for my father.

530
00:57:53.263 --> 00:57:58.947
One of them, Robert, worked for my father.

531
00:57:58.947 --> 00:58:10.316
My daddy’s other brother that lived local in this area, Rogers Braxton, he was a barber.

532
00:58:10.316 --> 00:58:23.105
I think at one point in time he was the oldest practicing barber in the state of Louisiana,

533
00:58:23.105 --> 00:58:33.053
in terms of the years he had been practicing, and quite possibly age, too.

534
00:58:33.053 --> 00:58:37.316
I’m not sure about the age.

535
00:58:37.316 --> 00:58:43.000
But he died at ninety-seven years old.

536
00:58:43.000 --> 00:58:45.623
<v DRAGOON>And he was in Mossville?

537
00:58:45.623 --> 00:58:48.770
<v BRAXTON>He was raised up in Mossville.

538
00:58:48.770 --> 00:58:54.541
He was . . . When he stopped cutting hair . . . He was cutting hair.

539
00:58:54.541 --> 00:58:57.689
He had a shop in Mossville.

540
00:58:57.689 --> 00:59:06.607
But prior to that, he had a shop in Lake Charles for a period of time, too.

541
00:59:06.607 --> 00:59:10.803
He used to cut my hair for free.

542
00:59:10.803 --> 00:59:16.574
I can recall, [Laughs] for free . . . all good things that’s free,

543
00:59:16.574 --> 00:59:27.066
not necessarily good things. [Laughs] At one point after I was working and grown, I was still going to him,

544
00:59:27.066 --> 00:59:30.738
because he was my uncle, for one,

545
00:59:30.738 --> 00:59:42.803
but he always wanted to cut my hair the way he wanted to cut my hair, not the way I wanted it cut.

546
00:59:42.803 --> 00:59:47.000
So I moved on. [Laughs] Let’s see . . .

547
00:59:47.000 --> 00:59:49.442
<v DRAGOON>Did most of the folks in Mossville . . .

548
00:59:49.442 --> 00:59:49.791
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

549
00:59:49.791 --> 00:59:55.023
<v DRAGOON>Did most of the folks in Mossville go to him to get their hair cut?

550
00:59:55.023 --> 00:59:55.372
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

551
00:59:55.372 --> 00:59:56.070
Oh, absolutely.

552
00:59:56.070 --> 00:59:57.116
Yeah, sure did.

553
00:59:57.116 --> 00:59:58.860
<v DRAGOON>Were there more than one?

554
00:59:58.860 --> 01:00:02.000
Were there other people, barber shops or barbers?

555
01:00:02.000 --> 01:00:17.429
<v BRAXTON>I think he . . . At the time, he had . . . they had a lot of people that cut hair bootleg.

556
01:00:17.429 --> 01:00:34.571
But real barber shop, I think he was the only one at the time . . . for the brief period in Mossville.

557
01:00:34.571 --> 01:00:42.286
But when he was . . . He cut many, many years.

558
01:00:42.286 --> 01:00:57.714
When I say . . . See, he cut hair for . . . I think it might have been approaching maybe sixty years.

559
01:00:57.714 --> 01:01:01.143
That’s a long time.

560
01:01:01.143 --> 01:01:06.286
<v DRAGOON>And what was his name again?

561
01:01:06.286 --> 01:01:08.000
<v BRAXTON>Rogers Braxton.

562
01:01:08.000 --> 01:01:12.286
Rogers, with an “s.” Rogers.

563
01:01:12.286 --> 01:01:22.571
And my mother’s . . . my father’s sister, Lily Verdine was her married name,

564
01:01:22.571 --> 01:01:32.857
she lived in Mossville right near us on the Old Spanish Trail.

565
01:01:32.857 --> 01:01:43.143
My grandmother, her father was white, so we all got that bloodline.

566
01:01:43.143 --> 01:01:45.714
And what else?

567
01:01:45.714 --> 01:01:56.000
I forgot what your . . . I might be straying from your question.

568
01:01:56.000 --> 01:01:59.286
<v CARHEEL>What was his name, her father?

569
01:01:59.286 --> 01:02:01.476
<v BRAXTON>Oh, I don’t recall.

570
01:02:01.476 --> 01:02:03.667
I don’t recall that.

571
01:02:03.667 --> 01:02:08.048
<v DRAGOON>Wait, you said your grandmother’s barber was white?

572
01:02:08.048 --> 01:02:09.143
<v BRAXTON>I’m sorry?

573
01:02:09.143 --> 01:02:10.238
<v CARHEEL>Her father.

574
01:02:10.238 --> 01:02:12.976
<v DRAGOON>Oh, her father was white.

575
01:02:12.976 --> 01:02:14.071
I’m sorry.

576
01:02:14.071 --> 01:02:15.167
I misheard.

577
01:02:15.167 --> 01:02:15.714
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

578
01:02:15.714 --> 01:02:19.000
My grandmother’s father was white.

579
01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:22.462
<v DRAGOON>Did she . . . Was that before she lived in Mossville?

580
01:02:22.462 --> 01:02:24.385
Was she born somewhere else?

581
01:02:24.385 --> 01:02:26.308
<v BRAXTON>She wasn’t born in Mossville.

582
01:02:26.308 --> 01:02:29.385
I think she was born in Beauregard Parish,

583
01:02:29.385 --> 01:02:30.154
<v DRAGOON>Oh, okay.

584
01:02:30.154 --> 01:02:34.000
Do you know what year she came to Mossville?

585
01:02:34.000 --> 01:02:39.554
<v BRAXTON>You know, I was trying to think of what year it was.

586
01:02:39.554 --> 01:02:52.051
I know my mother . . . I remember my mother telling me which one of them was a baby when they moved from Sugartown in Beauregard Parish to Mossville.

587
01:02:52.051 --> 01:02:55.291
But I really don’t remember the year.

588
01:02:55.291 --> 01:03:17.971
In fact, I didn’t realize that you was going to be making your appointment today because I had already thought some few days back that I was going to talk to one of my older cousins because I wanted to ask her that question of what year it was.

589
01:03:17.971 --> 01:03:21.211
I don’t recall what year it was.

590
01:03:21.211 --> 01:03:27.229
But it must have been . . . I don’t want to speculate when it was.

591
01:03:27.229 --> 01:03:33.246
<v DRAGOON>What kinds of stories . . . I know in the first interview you all did,

592
01:03:33.246 --> 01:03:45.743
you talked a lot about how your family talked a lot about lineage and you know a lot about going really far back in your family’s history.

593
01:03:45.743 --> 01:03:49.909
What kind of stories did your folks tell you?

594
01:03:49.909 --> 01:03:55.000
Did your parents tell you about your heritage, your ancestry?

595
01:03:55.000 --> 01:04:03.726
<v BRAXTON>You know, one thing about it as it connects to Mossville was that practically everybody was kin.

596
01:04:03.726 --> 01:04:07.319
I mean, they carried the last name.

597
01:04:07.319 --> 01:04:11.425
Moss, Mossville was named after some Mosses . . . Mossville.

598
01:04:11.425 --> 01:04:19.637
I’m not so sure that it picks up the name because the Mosses was the first,

599
01:04:19.637 --> 01:04:28.363
was permanent settlers here, or if it was just that Moss had the first post office here.

600
01:04:28.363 --> 01:04:37.602
And when you get a post office, you have to have a town designation to go with it.

601
01:04:37.602 --> 01:04:42.221
But what was your question? [Laughs] I don't know.

602
01:04:42.221 --> 01:04:44.788
My mind was . . . I’m deviating.

603
01:04:44.788 --> 01:04:45.814
<v DRAGOON>That’s okay.

604
01:04:45.814 --> 01:04:53.000
Just what kinds of stories would they tell you about your family heritage?

605
01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:53.728
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

606
01:04:53.728 --> 01:04:54.457
Stories.

607
01:04:54.457 --> 01:05:01.012
About the heritage itself . . . Now if you mean . . . Well,

608
01:05:01.012 --> 01:05:09.023
I know . . . You mean in terms of things that they experienced?

609
01:05:09.023 --> 01:05:09.751
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

610
01:05:09.751 --> 01:05:19.220
<v BRAXTON>I’m just trying to understand from which direction to bring these stories from.

611
01:05:19.220 --> 01:05:35.243
Because I mean, I’ve heard a lot of stories, but they . . . I mean, I don't know how they connect with heritage directly.

612
01:05:35.243 --> 01:05:52.723
For instance, my mother’s parents lived near Evergreen, what’s known as Evergreen Drive now, and I think the KCS [Kansas City Southern] Railroad tracks.

613
01:05:52.723 --> 01:05:57.821
They had . . . Because they were giving people,

614
01:05:57.821 --> 01:06:14.572
they had a reputation with hoboes where every hobo from here to wherever—hoboes had their own way of communicating—and they would feed hoboes,

615
01:06:14.572 --> 01:06:24.769
and they would give them chores to do to work for food and whatever.

616
01:06:24.769 --> 01:06:37.879
So hoboes used to frequent where they lived at because their property was adjacent to the railroad tracks.

617
01:06:37.879 --> 01:06:46.618
Is that what you’re talking about as far as heritage or so?

618
01:06:46.618 --> 01:06:52.445
And you know, they were farmers, my grandparents.

619
01:06:52.445 --> 01:06:59.000
So life was . . . it was a hard life.

620
01:06:59.000 --> 01:07:04.821
My grandmother on my mother’s side at one point in time,

621
01:07:04.821 --> 01:07:13.289
she used to ride a horse and buggy from Mossville to the sulphur mines in Sulphur.

622
01:07:13.289 --> 01:07:20.169
Sulphur got its name because it had a huge sulphur mining complex there.

623
01:07:20.169 --> 01:07:29.166
So there was a lot of people that stayed on site that was part of a compound.

624
01:07:29.166 --> 01:07:40.279
She used to ride there and do, I think, like housework and ironing and such while my grandfather worked the farm.

625
01:07:40.279 --> 01:07:51.393
In fact, one of the stories . . . one of my uncles and I, we were watching this movie one day, Blazing Saddles.

626
01:07:51.393 --> 01:07:57.744
It got to that part of the movie where, for whatever reason,

627
01:07:57.744 --> 01:08:10.445
one of the actors got fired up behind this horse and he hit that horse, [slaps hands] and he knocked that horse out cold.

628
01:08:10.445 --> 01:08:13.620
I said, “Boy that’s something else.”

629
01:08:13.620 --> 01:08:20.500
And so my uncle says, “You ought to see that happen for real.”

630
01:08:20.500 --> 01:08:23.146
I said, “What you mean?”

631
01:08:23.146 --> 01:08:26.851
He said, “Well, your grandfather did that.”

632
01:08:26.851 --> 01:08:42.727
He was plowing up potatoes and if he was working the horse past the end of the day . . . Animals have a way of knowing . . . you work an animal every day,

633
01:08:42.727 --> 01:08:53.312
they have a way of knowing when the end of a normal workday is; it’s the internal clock they have.

634
01:08:53.312 --> 01:09:03.367
So this old horse, he kept working the horse and the horse was ready to go to the stall.

635
01:09:03.367 --> 01:09:09.718
The horse was a little temperamental and he kicked at my grandfather.

636
01:09:09.718 --> 01:09:18.185
My grandfather dropped the reins and immediately went around to the side and caught the bridle.

637
01:09:18.185 --> 01:09:29.828
And my uncle says he hit him one time, and just knocked him out cold. [Laughs] But my father . . . You say heritage.

638
01:09:29.828 --> 01:09:42.000
If I’m going back from what I was told, not what I experienced, is that part of what you want to hear?

639
01:09:42.000 --> 01:09:42.357
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

640
01:09:42.357 --> 01:09:44.500
<v BRAXTON>Or you want my personal experiences?

641
01:09:44.500 --> 01:09:47.000
<v DRAGOON>Stories that you’ve heard or . . . yeah.

642
01:09:47.000 --> 01:09:55.609
<v BRAXTON>Well, one of the stories . . . In fact, I just wrote them about this stuff, because I write these memories.

643
01:09:55.609 --> 01:10:00.141
I have, probably two hundred of them on my phone.

644
01:10:00.141 --> 01:10:04.219
One was about . . . Because it was easy to write.

645
01:10:04.219 --> 01:10:11.016
My uncle and my father, my Uncle Rob and my father, had went to church.

646
01:10:11.016 --> 01:10:19.625
On their way home from church, it was basically through a wooded trail, and they came upon this skunk.

647
01:10:19.625 --> 01:10:23.703
Well, they knew better than messing with a skunk.

648
01:10:23.703 --> 01:10:31.406
But boys will be boys, they decide to mess with this skunk and the skunk sprayed them.

649
01:10:31.406 --> 01:10:34.125
And they wearing their Sunday clothes.

650
01:10:34.125 --> 01:10:40.922
And they basically, at the time that we’re talking about, back in the . . . before 1920,

651
01:10:40.922 --> 01:10:51.344
they only had two sets of clothes and that’s what they wore on Sunday and what they wore the rest of the week.

652
01:10:51.344 --> 01:10:59.953
So their clothes were all skunked up, and they knew that they was in trouble when they got home.

653
01:10:59.953 --> 01:11:10.375
So they decided that they was going to . . . they took all their clothes off and they got naked and they buried their clothes.

654
01:11:10.375 --> 01:11:18.531
They thought that within the seven days until Sunday come back again, they could do something about that.

655
01:11:18.531 --> 01:11:24.875
Somehow they was going to work around that problem of the clothes being smelly.

656
01:11:24.875 --> 01:11:28.047
Well, Sunday came around, and they didn’t.

657
01:11:28.047 --> 01:11:31.219
So they had to suffer the consequences.

658
01:11:31.219 --> 01:11:38.922
And they . . . he didn’t tell me what the consequences was, my father, who told me this story.

659
01:11:38.922 --> 01:11:45.603
But I’m sure it wasn’t nothing nice. [Laughs] They . . . My uncle, my aunt,

660
01:11:45.603 --> 01:11:54.452
my daddy’s sister and my uncle, during the time I was coming up, they fished every day.

661
01:11:54.452 --> 01:11:57.575
Aunt Lily Verdine fished every day.

662
01:11:57.575 --> 01:12:07.466
I recall going fishing with them sometimes in the Houston River in the boat . . . no motor, just paddle around.

663
01:12:07.466 --> 01:12:09.548
That’s all you needed.

664
01:12:09.548 --> 01:12:14.233
It was easy to catch fish in those days.

665
01:12:14.233 --> 01:12:17.356
It’s not like it is today.

666
01:12:17.356 --> 01:12:23.603
The water was much cleaner and the fish were much more plentiful.

667
01:12:23.603 --> 01:12:27.247
So they used to fish every day.

668
01:12:27.247 --> 01:12:38.699
So a lot of times me and my father would go down there on his time off and they would fry fish.

669
01:12:38.699 --> 01:12:41.301
That was a regular occasion.

670
01:12:41.301 --> 01:12:50.151
I learned how to eat a whole fish, say, six inches long, between two slices of bread,

671
01:12:50.151 --> 01:12:58.479
with all the bones in it . . . a fish sandwich full of bones . . . and never got choked,

672
01:12:58.479 --> 01:13:01.603
not a once on a bone.

673
01:13:01.603 --> 01:13:05.767
You just learn how to do certain things.

674
01:13:05.767 --> 01:13:12.534
I used to enjoy going fishing with them the times that I went.

675
01:13:12.534 --> 01:13:16.178
I guess my mother loved to fish.

676
01:13:16.178 --> 01:13:26.589
Of course my father died when I was ten, but as far as fishing went, it just kept rolling on,

677
01:13:26.589 --> 01:13:37.000
because my mother loved to fish and we used to fish all the time, as frequently as we could.

678
01:13:37.000 --> 01:13:45.283
<c.p>As far as . . . I heard so many stories, it’s hard to . . . my head is so full of stories.

679
01:13:45.283 --> 01:13:55.407
My uncles and such used to . . . I remember them telling me a story about they did a lot of work around Mossville.

680
01:13:55.407 --> 01:14:03.690
It was a church that existed already, but they were hired to put a steeple on this church.

681
01:14:03.690 --> 01:14:08.292
All the brothers were working on this one steeple installation.

682
01:14:08.292 --> 01:14:15.655
And we had this one uncle, we always thought of him as being a little absent-minded.

683
01:14:15.655 --> 01:14:24.858
He was a great carpenter, but he would have a million things at a time going on in his mind.

684
01:14:24.858 --> 01:14:33.602
He was working on the roof and they kept telling him, “Frederick, you’re about to back off that roof.

685
01:14:33.602 --> 01:14:34.522
Be careful.”

686
01:14:34.522 --> 01:14:41.425
He’d be a grouch box and he would say, “Yeah, I know where I’m at.”

687
01:14:41.425 --> 01:14:53.850
Anyway, he had this rope tied around him that was supposed to be a safety rope in case you accidentally back off or trip or fall, whatever.

688
01:14:53.850 --> 01:14:57.071
Well, he never tied his rope off.

689
01:14:57.071 --> 01:15:03.053
He pulled around . . . He had it tied off, but he never retied it.

690
01:15:03.053 --> 01:15:08.115
He flew on his back off the top of that church.

691
01:15:08.115 --> 01:15:14.558
And of course his rope was on him, but it was tied too long.

692
01:15:14.558 --> 01:15:21.000
He suffered the consequences of that fall with some broken bones and such.

693
01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:26.525
When I was a kid, we used to fish all over the area.

694
01:15:26.525 --> 01:15:33.325
There were a lot of ponds around the area that we used to go fishing at,

695
01:15:33.325 --> 01:15:38.850
besides going to the river, which was probably four or five miles away.

696
01:15:38.850 --> 01:15:46.925
There were ponds we could fish in and I used to fish in ponds a lot in the area.

697
01:15:46.925 --> 01:15:50.750
I used to hunt in the area a lot.

698
01:15:50.750 --> 01:15:56.275
I basically had to teach myself how to hunt because, like I said,

699
01:15:56.275 --> 01:16:02.225
my father died when I was ten, but I was up for the challenge.

700
01:16:02.225 --> 01:16:11.150
I remember a very, very . . . coincidental to me may be killing the very first animal I ever killed, was a rabbit.

701
01:16:11.150 --> 01:16:17.525
I killed it with a gun that my father had gave me before he passed.

702
01:16:17.525 --> 01:16:25.175
It just happened that that day the guys were getting off of work that worked for my mother.

703
01:16:25.175 --> 01:16:30.275
I was coming home with my rabbit, and I was very proud.

704
01:16:30.275 --> 01:16:32.400
Man, I was carrying him.

705
01:16:32.400 --> 01:16:34.950
When I passed by those guys,

706
01:16:34.950 --> 01:16:45.575
you could have sworn that I done killed a thousand rabbits before because I just walked right by them like it was no big thing.

707
01:16:45.575 --> 01:16:54.075
<c.p>[Laughs] So I wanted them to say something, though, but I didn’t want to say, “Hey, I killed a rabbit!”

708
01:16:54.075 --> 01:16:56.200
You know, “Look at me!”

709
01:16:56.200 --> 01:16:59.600
So my daddy’s brother, he called me back.

710
01:16:59.600 --> 01:17:01.300
He said, “Come see.”

711
01:17:01.300 --> 01:17:09.375
So I went over there and he took my rabbit and he felt around the stomach on the rabbit.

712
01:17:09.375 --> 01:17:12.775
Then they all start laughing, all the guys.

713
01:17:12.775 --> 01:17:20.000
I had killed a pregnant rabbit. [Laughs] So he told me, he said, “You can’t eat that.”

714
01:17:20.000 --> 01:17:23.400
The flesh and everything is different on them.

715
01:17:23.400 --> 01:17:28.075
So that was my very first kill was a pregnant rabbit.

716
01:17:28.075 --> 01:17:37.000
I just thought it was a big rabbit. [laughs] I wasn’t but about eleven years old, ten, eleven years old.

717
01:17:37.000 --> 01:17:47.744
We used to find time . . . we used to fill out the time with all kinds of things as a kid in Mossville.

718
01:17:47.744 --> 01:17:49.698
The grass never grew.

719
01:17:49.698 --> 01:18:02.395
Anybody that had a couple of kids, they didn’t have grass in the yard because every kid played outside and the grass just would not grow.

720
01:18:02.395 --> 01:18:11.674
I don’t care what you did, it was just so much activity outside, the grass just would not grow.

721
01:18:11.674 --> 01:18:15.581
It would grow in outlying spots or whatever.

722
01:18:15.581 --> 01:18:22.907
But we used to find all sorts . . . It was never a dull moment, you know.

723
01:18:22.907 --> 01:18:38.047
If you weren’t making a slingshot or making your own kite to fly or . . . one of the things we used to do a lot is just go walk in the woods.

724
01:18:38.047 --> 01:18:44.884
We would just go walk in the woods, walk through the woods and explore,

725
01:18:44.884 --> 01:18:51.721
we’d call it see what we could see or find or experience or whatever.

726
01:18:51.721 --> 01:19:01.977
I can recall a real big experience in my life was the first time I recall seeing . . . watching a color movie.

727
01:19:01.977 --> 01:19:05.395
We had a black and white TV.

728
01:19:05.395 --> 01:19:11.256
But during the summer they would show movies at the recreation center.

729
01:19:11.256 --> 01:19:16.140
What do you call it, sixteen millimeter videos or whatever?

730
01:19:16.140 --> 01:19:19.070
Or eight millimeter, whatever they are?

731
01:19:19.070 --> 01:19:27.860
And the first movie I went to see was . . . I’m trying to think of the name of it.

732
01:19:27.860 --> 01:19:29.326
It was . . . Aladdin?

733
01:19:29.326 --> 01:19:31.767
I think it was Aladdin.

734
01:19:31.767 --> 01:19:33.721
It was in color.

735
01:19:33.721 --> 01:19:36.163
Boy, that was something else.

736
01:19:36.163 --> 01:19:42.512
Once you’ve seen a color movie on a big screen, that was fascinating.

737
01:19:42.512 --> 01:19:47.395
That was something that you just longed to see again.

738
01:19:47.395 --> 01:19:50.814
But it wasn’t something that came often.

739
01:19:50.814 --> 01:20:04.000
In the summer, they might have showed two or three movies, but those two or three movies was quite an experience, even for your whole summer.

740
01:20:04.000 --> 01:20:09.250
<v DRAGOON>What year did they get the screen, the projector?

741
01:20:09.250 --> 01:20:11.000
<v BRAXTON>The what?

742
01:20:11.000 --> 01:20:12.824
<v DRAGOON>Or the television?

743
01:20:12.824 --> 01:20:20.730
What year did the rec center, were they able to show color movies?

744
01:20:20.730 --> 01:20:22.554
<v BRAXTON>On the television?

745
01:20:22.554 --> 01:20:26.203
I don't think on the television.

746
01:20:26.203 --> 01:20:29.243
<v CARHEEL>She said on the screen.

747
01:20:29.243 --> 01:20:41.405
<v BRAXTON>Oh, on the . . . It was the middle of the ‘60s when we seen this . . . probably ’65, 1965 or so, thereabouts.

748
01:20:41.405 --> 01:20:49.919
But I don't think that was like early for projection type movie . . . eight millimeter.

749
01:20:49.919 --> 01:20:55.392
I think that was available probably sometime before that.

750
01:20:55.392 --> 01:21:09.378
But color TV didn’t come along until . . . it wasn’t out there for everybody to purchase until sometime toward the end of the ‘60s,

751
01:21:09.378 --> 01:21:11.203
I would imagine.

752
01:21:11.203 --> 01:21:13.027
I don’t recall.

753
01:21:13.027 --> 01:21:23.973
I don't think . . . I don't know that I had a color TV, personally, up until in the ‘70s,

754
01:21:23.973 --> 01:21:33.095
I think, is when I first got a color television set . . . the early ‘70s, yeah.

755
01:21:33.095 --> 01:21:41.000
<v CARHEEL>Did you ask where did they get their projector and stuff from?

756
01:21:41.000 --> 01:21:44.200
<v DRAGOON>Oh, do you know where they got it from?

757
01:21:44.200 --> 01:21:44.556
<v CARHEEL>Oh.

758
01:21:44.556 --> 01:21:49.178
No, I don’t. [Laughs] I would imagine the school, but I don't know.

759
01:21:49.178 --> 01:21:49.889
<v DRAGOON>Oh, okay.

760
01:21:49.889 --> 01:21:57.000
So they’d have a big like movie event where a lot of people would come to the rec center?

761
01:21:57.000 --> 01:22:06.524
<v BRAXTON>Well, no. It was, like I say, it was just on occasion, maybe two or three times during the summer.

762
01:22:06.524 --> 01:22:10.810
And it was just a small screen . . . indoors projection.

763
01:22:10.810 --> 01:22:17.476
But now, we did go through the drive-ins; you could go through the drive-ins.

764
01:22:17.476 --> 01:22:29.857
They had indoor movie theaters, too, but the drive-in theaters were real popular and there used to be two or three of them in the area.

765
01:22:29.857 --> 01:22:32.238
When I was a kid,

766
01:22:32.238 --> 01:22:45.571
we used to go sneak in sometimes where the grass was tall . . . crawl in. and find your speaker in an outlying area and drop it to the ground,

767
01:22:45.571 --> 01:22:49.381
just lay down there and watch the movie.

768
01:22:49.381 --> 01:22:51.286
Otherwise, you’d actually pay.

769
01:22:51.286 --> 01:22:54.619
I mean, that was the normal route.

770
01:22:54.619 --> 01:23:01.762
You’d pay so much a carload and you’d go in and you would watch movies.

771
01:23:01.762 --> 01:23:05.095
We used to do that quite often.

772
01:23:05.095 --> 01:23:11.762
But for some reason, that was . . . I say quite often might be an exaggeration,

773
01:23:11.762 --> 01:23:17.000
but we used to go to the movies at times.

774
01:23:17.000 --> 01:23:20.111
<v DRAGOON>Which one . . . which drive-in would you go to?

775
01:23:20.111 --> 01:23:29.444
<v BRAXTON>We had two that was really prominent during my time and that was the Midway Drive-in and the New Moon Drive-in in Lake Charles.

776
01:23:29.444 --> 01:23:31.000
<v DRAGOON>The New Moon?

777
01:23:31.000 --> 01:23:42.375
<v BRAXTON>The Midway was within bicycle riding distance, you might say . . . about a mile and a half from where we lived at.

778
01:23:42.375 --> 01:23:46.167
The New Moon was a different story.

779
01:23:46.167 --> 01:23:52.125
That was in Lake Charles, probably six or seven miles away.

780
01:23:52.125 --> 01:23:59.708
So the Midway was the one that we went to most of the time.

781
01:23:59.708 --> 01:24:05.125
<v DRAGOON>How old were you when you’d go to the drive-in?

782
01:24:05.125 --> 01:24:10.000
Was it in your teenage years or younger?

783
01:24:10.000 --> 01:24:15.053
<v BRAXTON>Younger than the teens and into adult life.

784
01:24:15.053 --> 01:24:28.316
I mean, my kids, actually, some of . . . my youngest daughter, actually, experienced going to the drive-in before they closed down completely.

785
01:24:28.316 --> 01:24:34.000
<v DRAGOON>So it was there for quite a while?

786
01:24:34.000 --> 01:24:41.307
<v BRAXTON>I think the last one closed somewhere around maybe 1980 or thereabouts.

787
01:24:41.307 --> 01:24:44.961
That’s when the last one closed.

788
01:24:44.961 --> 01:24:52.877
I’m just full of stories, but I can’t think of none about myself.

789
01:24:52.877 --> 01:25:08.101
You know, some of the stories that were passed on to me was . . . my mother used to tell me about my uncles . . . Frederick and Johnny,

790
01:25:08.101 --> 01:25:12.972
in particular, because they was the two youngest.

791
01:25:12.972 --> 01:25:16.017
They were younger than her.

792
01:25:16.017 --> 01:25:21.497
They used to live and dream working with horses.

793
01:25:21.497 --> 01:25:42.201
They used to literally dream about horses at night and actually they slept in the same bed and they would actually end up inside of each other’s dreams and they would act them out.

794
01:25:42.201 --> 01:25:51.335
One would be on top of the other in the bed hollering, “Get him, Johnny!

795
01:25:51.335 --> 01:25:53.162
Get him, Johnny!”

796
01:25:53.162 --> 01:25:56.816
“All right, I got him, Bigby!”

797
01:25:56.816 --> 01:26:01.687
And they’d be dead asleep in their dreams.

798
01:26:01.687 --> 01:26:23.000
My mother says she used to beat on the wall and sometimes she’d have to go around to the room to break them up and tell them wake up and go back to sleep.

799
01:26:23.000 --> 01:26:31.911
My youngest uncle, Johnny, he had a sister named [Burgundy?] that died at a young age.

800
01:26:31.911 --> 01:26:39.150
But when they used to have open wells, it was just an open,

801
01:26:39.150 --> 01:26:58.085
deep pit well where you had a structure built over on top of a well with a pulley and you would drop a big old two or three-gallon bucket down there to retrieve water.

802
01:26:58.085 --> 01:27:12.565
And he was just a little bitty kid when they had a wall built up around this opening that was probably three or four feet tall.

803
01:27:12.565 --> 01:27:25.931
And he was a little bit short to be trying to work over that wall, especially when you got a heavy bucket of water.

804
01:27:25.931 --> 01:27:38.183
He went out to get water and the weight of the bucket and everything got the best of him and pulled him.

805
01:27:38.183 --> 01:27:40.967
He fell down the well.

806
01:27:40.967 --> 01:28:00.459
Just luckily, his sister [Burgundy?] seen him because these wells were built with ladders going down them because you had to clean them out real frequently from all the leaves and debris falling in them.

807
01:28:00.459 --> 01:28:18.280
Well, she was able to climb that ladder and go down and get him and save him because she was older than he was and she carried him out of the well.

808
01:28:18.280 --> 01:28:24.963
She died not too long after that with scarlet fever, I believe.

809
01:28:24.963 --> 01:28:30.533
All my mother’s other sisters and brothers lived long lives.

810
01:28:30.533 --> 01:28:34.988
That’s the only one that died very young.

811
01:28:34.988 --> 01:28:35.545
Break?

812
01:28:35.545 --> 01:28:37.215
Break, break, break?

813
01:28:37.215 --> 01:28:38.886
Can you break?

814
01:28:38.886 --> 01:28:40.000
<v DRAGOON>Sure.

815
01:28:40.000 --> 01:28:41.068
All righty.

816
01:28:41.068 --> 01:28:45.874
We took a quick break and now we’re back.

817
01:28:45.874 --> 01:28:48.544
Coming back with a story.

818
01:28:48.544 --> 01:28:54.951
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, I was . . . My father also had a brother named Albert Braxton.

819
01:28:54.951 --> 01:28:57.621
He was born in 1897.

820
01:28:57.621 --> 01:29:08.301
When he was fourteen years old, he was walking home from church with a group of kids, boys and girls.

821
01:29:08.301 --> 01:29:18.981
And one of the girls that was in the party was a former girlfriend that he still had feelings for,

822
01:29:18.981 --> 01:29:22.718
but she was walking with another guy.

823
01:29:22.718 --> 01:29:24.854
Let me correct something.

824
01:29:24.854 --> 01:29:30.728
He was not fourteen years old, he was seventeen years old.

825
01:29:30.728 --> 01:29:34.466
So anyway, they got into a quarrel.

826
01:29:34.466 --> 01:29:45.146
He and the guy got into a quarrel and he hit the guy in the face with a ceramic jug,

827
01:29:45.146 --> 01:29:53.155
similar to what you used to see Granny drink out of on the Beverly Hillbillies.

828
01:29:53.155 --> 01:30:01.699
And he seriously fractured . . . damaged this guy’s frontal face area and drove bones into his brain.

829
01:30:01.699 --> 01:30:09.175
To make a long story short, then he died a couple of days later.

830
01:30:09.175 --> 01:30:19.854
My uncle Albert, he realized this guy was really sick and probably going to die and he fled . . . very young,

831
01:30:19.854 --> 01:30:21.990
not to be found.

832
01:30:21.990 --> 01:30:26.796
The sheriff didn’t catch him till twenty-five years later.

833
01:30:26.796 --> 01:30:39.612
He had got word by somebody that went to Houston, Texas, where he was at and saw him and snitched back to the sheriff.

834
01:30:39.612 --> 01:30:47.087
The sheriff went and picked him up and brought him back to Lake Charles.

835
01:30:47.087 --> 01:30:52.961
And a few months after that returned, he had his trial.

836
01:30:52.961 --> 01:31:00.437
During the trial, they ended up charging him with a suspended sentence for manslaughter.

837
01:31:00.437 --> 01:31:09.515
He was seventeen years old at the time of the incident so he got a suspended sentence.

838
01:31:09.515 --> 01:31:14.854
He got charged with manslaughter and he was set free.

839
01:31:14.854 --> 01:31:25.000
He had an illness as well and the judge took all that in consideration and set him free.

840
01:31:25.000 --> 01:31:32.817
Well, after he was set free in the early part of 1939,

841
01:31:32.817 --> 01:31:47.799
I found where he had actually lived in the same house with my father and his brother and my mother and her mother.

842
01:31:47.799 --> 01:31:56.919
And her mother, Velma’s mother, at the time was only about six months old.

843
01:31:56.919 --> 01:32:02.782
And that’s all I know about that particular uncle.

844
01:32:02.782 --> 01:32:11.250
He died some few years after that, back long before I was born.

845
01:32:11.250 --> 01:32:22.975
My . . . I had another uncle, Uncle Melvin, on my father’s side of the family that lived in Houston.

846
01:32:22.975 --> 01:32:31.444
Out of all of my daddy’s sisters and brothers, even though I knew,

847
01:32:31.444 --> 01:32:43.169
I spent the least amount of time with him, I was closer to him than all the rest.

848
01:32:43.169 --> 01:32:49.032
We just happened to just really make it together.

849
01:32:49.032 --> 01:32:52.940
But he died in like 1982.

850
01:32:52.940 --> 01:32:56.197
He was in his mid-eighties.

851
01:32:56.197 --> 01:33:04.014
My Uncle Rob died— that’s my daddy’s youngest brother—in the mid ‘70s.

852
01:33:04.014 --> 01:33:06.620
His name was Robert.

853
01:33:06.620 --> 01:33:17.694
On my mother’s side of the family, the uncle that I was the closest to was Johnny.

854
01:33:17.694 --> 01:33:22.905
We actually spent a lot of time together.

855
01:33:22.905 --> 01:33:27.465
He passed on a few years back.

856
01:33:27.465 --> 01:33:32.676
But Johnny was my fishing buddy, hunting buddy.

857
01:33:32.676 --> 01:33:36.585
We just socialized at home together.

858
01:33:36.585 --> 01:33:39.190
We were very close.

859
01:33:39.190 --> 01:33:46.356
He was the youngest of them brothers that was all carpenters.

860
01:33:46.356 --> 01:33:58.732
So I learned some things from him about carpentry, even though I never did a lot of carpenter work.

861
01:33:58.732 --> 01:34:05.898
But just being around him, I learned quite a few things.

862
01:34:05.898 --> 01:34:14.366
Most of my mother’s other brothers and sisters all lived . . . moved to California.

863
01:34:14.366 --> 01:34:27.394
One moved to California when I was very young, in the middle of the ‘60s, Etta, and she lived there.

864
01:34:27.394 --> 01:34:30.000
She died there.

865
01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:32.848
<v DRAGOON>Why did they move to California?

866
01:34:32.848 --> 01:34:33.323
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

867
01:34:33.323 --> 01:34:36.172
<v DRAGOON>Why did they move to California?

868
01:34:36.172 --> 01:34:41.869
<v BRAXTON>We had a lot of family members, a lot of family members,

869
01:34:41.869 --> 01:34:48.990
around 1965 or thereabouts, they went to California because that was the land of opportunity.

870
01:34:48.990 --> 01:34:56.111
The ones that moved there pretty much lived out the rest of their life there.

871
01:34:56.111 --> 01:34:58.960
On both sides of the family.

872
01:34:58.960 --> 01:35:09.404
On my mother’s side, the Towners, and from my father’s side, the Braxtons, had a lot of them that moved to California.

873
01:35:09.404 --> 01:35:17.000
<v DRAGOON>And that was right at the height of the Civil Rights Movement, in the ‘60s?

874
01:35:17.000 --> 01:35:23.160
<v BRAXTON>It was right before the height of the Civil Rights Movement.

875
01:35:23.160 --> 01:35:28.200
Well, I mark that as like 1968 or thereabouts.

876
01:35:28.200 --> 01:35:35.480
But most of them was prior to that time, the ’64, ’65 time.

877
01:35:35.480 --> 01:35:46.120
Actually, even some of them earlier than that . . . the early to the mid ‘60s when they moved to California.

878
01:35:46.120 --> 01:35:52.280
Well, like I said, my mother’s sister Etta moved to California.

879
01:35:52.280 --> 01:36:00.120
And when I say she moved, she moved her and all of her kids.

880
01:36:00.120 --> 01:36:06.280
So that was a whole family that just moved to California.

881
01:36:06.280 --> 01:36:13.560
And she had grown kids that had kids and they moved to California.

882
01:36:13.560 --> 01:36:23.640
So it was quite a number of people that moved to California on both sides of the family.

883
01:36:23.640 --> 01:36:38.200
My mother’s brother Franklin Towner, he was one of the ones that moved in the same timeframe, but he only went as far as Houston, basically.

884
01:36:38.200 --> 01:36:46.600
He lived most of his life in Houston after that, and raised a family there.

885
01:36:46.600 --> 01:36:51.080
And the same thing with her brother Frederick.

886
01:36:51.080 --> 01:36:52.200
He moved.

887
01:36:52.200 --> 01:37:00.040
That’s how I ended up pulling all those nails that I talked about earlier.

888
01:37:00.040 --> 01:37:09.000
He moved to the Katy, Texas area and lived the rest of his life there.

889
01:37:09.000 --> 01:37:13.941
Of course my grandmother lived right across the street from us, my mother’s mother.

890
01:37:13.941 --> 01:37:19.588
That’s how I learned to whistle from my grandmother, because she used to whistle at me.

891
01:37:19.588 --> 01:37:30.529
She used to sit in the window and she could see across the street and whenever she saw us doing something that just didn’t look right to her from a distance,

892
01:37:30.529 --> 01:37:31.941
[whistles] she would whistle.

893
01:37:31.941 --> 01:37:34.765
And I’d say, “How does she do that?”

894
01:37:34.765 --> 01:37:39.353
Because most people would whistle [whistles quietly] but she could whistle loud, man!

895
01:37:39.353 --> 01:37:42.882
I say, “I have to learn how to do that.”

896
01:37:42.882 --> 01:37:50.647
That was one of the things . . . You didn’t have much to think about when you was a kid a lot of times.

897
01:37:50.647 --> 01:38:00.176
So we used to find these little pieces of metal scraps they used to use on the railroad and they would have little tiny holes in it.

898
01:38:00.176 --> 01:38:05.824
It would be like the size . . . like if you were going to a concert or something,

899
01:38:05.824 --> 01:38:09.000
they’d give you one of those little small tickets.

900
01:38:09.000 --> 01:38:17.118
It was a little piece of metal about an inch wide, an inch and a half long and it had holes in it.

901
01:38:17.118 --> 01:38:23.471
If you fold the holes over, fold the metal over to where the holes align on each side,

902
01:38:23.471 --> 01:38:28.059
you could put it in your mouth and you could make a whistle.

903
01:38:28.059 --> 01:38:30.882
We used to make those all the time.

904
01:38:30.882 --> 01:38:32.294
Do you remember those?

905
01:38:32.294 --> 01:38:33.000
<v CARHEEL>Yes.

906
01:38:33.000 --> 01:38:35.467
<v BRAXTON>We used to make those all the time.

907
01:38:35.467 --> 01:38:36.083
<v CARHEEL>My first [ . . . ?]

908
01:38:36.083 --> 01:38:38.858
<v BRAXTON>But I could make a loud whistle with that.

909
01:38:38.858 --> 01:38:40.708
But that still wasn’t the same.

910
01:38:40.708 --> 01:38:41.942
She didn’t use anything!

911
01:38:41.942 --> 01:38:43.175
It took me forever.

912
01:38:43.175 --> 01:38:49.342
I never would ask her how to do it, but I see her do it if I was close by.

913
01:38:49.342 --> 01:38:54.892
If I see her whistling at somebody, I would kind of look at her, how she do it.

914
01:38:54.892 --> 01:38:56.433
And I tried and tried.

915
01:38:56.433 --> 01:39:02.600
It took me a long time, but I learned how to perfect it to where if I whistle loud enough,

916
01:39:02.600 --> 01:39:05.992
I hear my own ears rattle from the sound of it.

917
01:39:05.992 --> 01:39:06.608
<v CARHEEL>Do it.

918
01:39:06.608 --> 01:39:07.533
Let her see.

919
01:39:07.533 --> 01:39:08.767
<v BRAXTON>Oh, I just did.

920
01:39:08.767 --> 01:39:10.000
<v CARHEEL>That wasn’t loud.

921
01:39:10.000 --> 01:39:12.360
<v BRAXTON>[whistles] Just a little bit.

922
01:39:12.360 --> 01:39:15.663
But I owe that to my grandmother.

923
01:39:15.663 --> 01:39:21.326
I guess that’s . . . I do that quite often, I whistle at people.

924
01:39:21.326 --> 01:39:26.517
It’s just a habit, I guess, to get their attention only.

925
01:39:26.517 --> 01:39:29.820
But my grandmother was a real disciplinarian.

926
01:39:29.820 --> 01:39:35.955
She come from the days when everybody, all adults, were heavy into discipline.

927
01:39:35.955 --> 01:39:45.393
But I can recall when I used to go over there, she used to talk a lot about the rapture.

928
01:39:45.393 --> 01:39:49.169
She was really into the Bible a lot.

929
01:39:49.169 --> 01:39:51.528
Read her Bible every day.

930
01:39:51.528 --> 01:39:57.663
I used to think, wow, you know, the rapture might be here tomorrow.

931
01:39:57.663 --> 01:40:08.989
It was put to you in such a way that you really start feeling like somehow she knew the rapture was fixing to happen.

932
01:40:08.989 --> 01:40:12.292
It was going to be real close.

933
01:40:12.292 --> 01:40:14.652
You know what I’m saying?

934
01:40:14.652 --> 01:40:19.843
And that’s the way they used to do it back then.

935
01:40:19.843 --> 01:40:22.674
They used to talk about it.

936
01:40:22.674 --> 01:40:34.000
I think they did that on purpose to make you think that you better straighten up now because the rapture is right close.

937
01:40:34.000 --> 01:40:39.294
In fact, I have a cousin, my daddy’s first cousin, Bud Lyons.

938
01:40:39.294 --> 01:40:47.235
When he was a young man, I understand around the age of sixteen years old, eighteen years old,

939
01:40:47.235 --> 01:40:54.735
he married a lady that was around thirty-six years old and she was very, very, very religious.

940
01:40:54.735 --> 01:40:59.588
She got him the same way, I guess you might say.

941
01:40:59.588 --> 01:41:03.559
He was really into the Bible and the scripture.

942
01:41:03.559 --> 01:41:08.853
For some reason, this thing around the rapture was what everybody talked.

943
01:41:08.853 --> 01:41:12.824
So that was dwelled upon a lot, the rapture.

944
01:41:12.824 --> 01:41:19.441
Well, now you got to realize this was like back in the 1920s, I think.

945
01:41:19.441 --> 01:41:25.618
You know, we didn’t have television . . . well, a lot of people didn’t have radios.

946
01:41:25.618 --> 01:41:29.588
Most people didn’t have radios back . . . Nobody had electricity.

947
01:41:29.588 --> 01:41:30.029
Okay?

948
01:41:30.029 --> 01:41:33.559
So he was into the scripture and rapture.

949
01:41:33.559 --> 01:41:38.853
He was outside cutting wood one day and he heard this sound.

950
01:41:38.853 --> 01:41:44.147
He looked up and he saw this thing in the air coming.

951
01:41:44.147 --> 01:41:50.324
In his mind, what he saw was the same way angels had been described.

952
01:41:50.324 --> 01:42:02.676
So he automatically in his mind thought the rapture was fixing to take place and the angels was coming down and he didn’t want to get left behind.

953
01:42:02.676 --> 01:42:09.735
So he tried to get the attention of this thing he saw flying, and he couldn’t.

954
01:42:09.735 --> 01:42:31.353
So he ended up climbing on top of the house for a higher vantage point and he became so filled with the belief that he was going to ascend into the heavens with the rapture that he just jumped off the house and thought he was going to ascend.

955
01:42:31.353 --> 01:42:32.676
Well, he didn’t.

956
01:42:32.676 --> 01:42:34.441
He hit the ground.

957
01:42:34.441 --> 01:42:37.529
He broke an arm and a leg.

958
01:42:37.529 --> 01:42:49.000
He later found out that was an airplane he saw. [Laughs] That was the first airplane he ever seen in his life. [Laughs] You know?

959
01:42:49.000 --> 01:42:50.901
<v DRAGOON>What year was that?

960
01:42:50.901 --> 01:42:52.326
Do you know?

961
01:42:52.326 --> 01:42:55.653
<v BRAXTON>This was back like in the ‘20s.

962
01:42:55.653 --> 01:42:58.504
Like in the ‘20s, late ‘20s.

963
01:42:58.504 --> 01:43:02.781
And you know, that’s how life was back then.

964
01:43:02.781 --> 01:43:08.959
It was . . . a lot of mysterious things happening, I guess you might say.

965
01:43:08.959 --> 01:43:14.661
I know my uncle telling me that this was a big deal.

966
01:43:14.661 --> 01:43:24.165
Somewhere on the west coast, in California, the Pacific coast I’ll say, anyway, this whale had beached itself and died.

967
01:43:24.165 --> 01:43:29.393
And somehow this guy . . . industrial . . . What’s the word I’m looking for?

968
01:43:29.393 --> 01:43:34.145
Well anyway, he saw a way to make a buck.

969
01:43:34.145 --> 01:43:39.847
So he ended up getting this whale on some railroad flat cars.

970
01:43:39.847 --> 01:43:42.698
And people hadn’t seen a whale.

971
01:43:42.698 --> 01:43:44.599
People didn’t have TVs.

972
01:43:44.599 --> 01:43:46.975
They’d probably heard about it.

973
01:43:46.975 --> 01:43:56.955
So he put this thing on railcars from way in California and brought it as far this way as he could,

974
01:43:56.955 --> 01:44:05.508
stopping all along the way in select places, and charging people to see what a whale looked like.

975
01:44:05.508 --> 01:44:13.587
And by the time he got here, my uncle say, he told me where it was at,

976
01:44:13.587 --> 01:44:24.517
on Prater Road and Highway 90 where they stopped at, he said the whale was rotten and pieces was falling off the car.

977
01:44:24.517 --> 01:44:31.645
But it was still identifiable enough that you could tell it was a huge fish.

978
01:44:31.645 --> 01:44:41.624
And people was paying . . . he had to pay fifty cents [Laughs] to go stand out there and look at that thing.

979
01:44:41.624 --> 01:44:44.000
But that was entertainment.

980
01:44:44.000 --> 01:44:44.618
<v CARHEEL>Who did this?

981
01:44:44.618 --> 01:44:45.029
<v BRAXTON>Uncle Johnny.

982
01:44:45.029 --> 01:44:45.235
<v CARHEEL>Oh.

983
01:44:45.235 --> 01:44:48.529
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, he mentioned that several times . . . for different reasons he brought that up, going down there.

984
01:44:48.529 --> 01:44:51.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you know what year that happened or about what time?

985
01:44:51.000 --> 01:44:54.627
<v BRAXTON>It was when he was a young man.

986
01:44:54.627 --> 01:45:05.053
I would venture to say that he must have been in his twenties or somewhere in that area from what he told me.

987
01:45:05.053 --> 01:45:14.573
Because kind of at the same time, he was talking about my mother and my father and when they were courting.

988
01:45:14.573 --> 01:45:24.093
He remembered when they was courting because my mother and father got married when . . . well, her mother was the first born.

989
01:45:24.093 --> 01:45:31.347
And my mother was like . . . well, it was right at . . . they almost got the same birthday.

990
01:45:31.347 --> 01:45:35.427
So if she was right at twenty-three years old,

991
01:45:35.427 --> 01:45:45.400
and he’s a couple of years younger than her and that’s what the conversation we was having about at the same time,

992
01:45:45.400 --> 01:45:48.573
and one thing just led to another.

993
01:45:48.573 --> 01:45:53.560
So I would say, it probably was in the ‘30s, probably.

994
01:45:53.560 --> 01:45:54.920
<v CARHEEL>The mid ‘30s.

995
01:45:54.920 --> 01:45:59.000
<v BRAXTON>It was probably in the mid ‘30s, thereabouts.

996
01:45:59.000 --> 01:45:59.778
<v DRAGOON>All right.

997
01:45:59.778 --> 01:46:03.667
Well, we are at about an hour and forty-five [minutes].

998
01:46:03.667 --> 01:46:07.944
If you all feel like this is a good stopping point,

999
01:46:07.944 --> 01:46:15.333
and if you want to schedule another interview for, maybe in a couple of weeks, or something like that?

1000
01:46:15.333 --> 01:46:20.000
<v BRAXTON>If you want to stop now, unless you had another question.

1001
01:46:20.000 --> 01:46:20.333
<c.p>[End 4489.

1002
01:46:20.333 --> 01:46:20.833
End Session I.]
